encyclopedia :taluri do
  name "Taluri"
  summary "Taluri are small canopy gliders of Istrava, carrying pollen on fringed throats and filling hollow branches with soft stolen fibers."
  kind :lifeform
  subkind :animal
  status :complete
  topics :ecology, :household, :subject_planetary_life
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when all: { place: [:outer_system, :surface] }, any: { place: [:hot, :garden] }
  biology "Four-limbed gliders with a folding flank membrane, hooked digits and a brush of pollen-catching throat filaments"
  principal_accommodation "Connected warm canopy with flowering trees and sheltered nesting hollows"
  descriptive_identity appearance: "Folded membranes resemble two dry leaves against a narrow body; the throat brush turns yellow with pollen.", behavior: "Taluri glide between flowering boughs, return to familiar hollows and tug loose fibers into nests.", senses: "Foot contact detects vibration through a branch; large approaching steps send the animal to an opening on the far side."
  prose <<~PROSE
    A resting taluri presses its flank membranes flat and resembles a pair of dead leaves caught against the bark. It opens them to glide between crowns, then climbs with hooked digits. Its long throat brush reaches the flowers beneath #{encyclopedia_ref :ulessa} leaf rings and carries pollen to the next tree. Taluri are especially familiar around #{ref :iridess} and the older orchards of #{ref :naloven}.

    Family groups sleep in hollow boughs. They line the chambers with soft bark, loose fibers and anything light enough to pull through the entrance. A gardener can find a missing cuff, a strip of painted ribbon and a rival household's curtain fringe packed together above the path. Opening the chamber while its occupants are inside usually sends them out through a second socket. A blocked exit provokes a rush of teeth and hooked feet.

    Branch vibration warns taluri before a large climber appears. Keepers approach slowly, pause when a throat brush rises and offer a fresh flowering twig away from the nest. The #{ref :ilvaren, "Ilvaren"}'s drawings pair nests with the feeding trees around them; a decorative hollow in an isolated courtyard often stands empty despite an owner's repeated purchases. In crowded forest crowns, a returning glider may lead an attentive climber toward a dry refuge beyond the visible path.
  PROSE
  cue "Two dead leaves unfold into a glider, showing a bright pollen brush beneath its chin."
  cue "A colored thread disappears through a branch socket while tiny hooked feet brace against the bark."
  affordance "Watching returning taluri reveals occupied hollows and a canopy route between flowering trees."
  variation "Orchard groups tolerate quiet familiar gardeners and gather conspicuous domestic fibers."
  variation "Forest groups nest through several linked bough cavities, escaping an opened chamber into the neighboring crown."
end
